[center][size=6][b]Faster Loadin and Savin[/b][/size]
[size=4]A maintained 0BSD fork of Save and Load Accelerator for SKSE Cosaves[/size][/center]

[quote][b]Credit and provenance[/b]
This is a maintenance fork of [url=https://github.com/just-harry/save-load-accelerator-for-skse-cosaves]Save and Load Accelerator for SKSE Cosaves[/url], originally created by Harry Gillanders (just-harry). The public name was changed to distinguish the fork. The original code and the fork's modifications remain licensed under 0BSD.

Fork source: [url=https://github.com/ShugokiFable/Faster-Loadin-n-Savin]https://github.com/ShugokiFable/Faster-Loadin-n-Savin[/url][/quote]

[size=4][b][u]What is new in 1.5.1[/u][/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Real CPU selection in the FOMOD.[/b] Universal x86-64-v2 is recommended. High-End x86-64-v3 is an optional AVX2-class build and overlays only the selected game version.
[*][b]Sanitized build metadata.[/b] User-specific absolute PDB paths are no longer exposed in the public DLLs.
[*][b]Reproducible release packaging.[/b] The release script now matches the public archive layout, validates all eight game targets and both CPU profiles, rejects development artifacts, and generates hashes and a DLL manifest.
[*][b]Clearer safety language.[/b] Atomic replacement reduces the risk of a truncated or destroyed SKSE cosave, but it is not an atomic transaction with Skyrim's main .ess file.
[*][b]Clearer verification language.[/b] Static import inspection is useful evidence, not absolute proof of every capability a native binary could exercise.
[*][b]Explicit credits and permissions guidance.[/b] The repository and Nexus permissions are intended to remain consistent with 0BSD.
[*][b]No save/load behavior change from 1.4.0 or 1.5.0.[/b] The 1.5.1 DLLs contain the same executable code and are restamped with corrected release metadata.
[/list]

[size=4][b][u]What the plugin does[/u][/b][/size]
This plugin accelerates the saving and loading of [b]SKSE cosave files[/b]. It does not replace or rewrite Skyrim's main .ess save format.

SKSE plugins serialize data through many small API calls. This plugin patches SKSE's cosave routines at runtime so plugin data is assembled in memory and written more efficiently.

The maintenance fork also changes the disk path used by its optimized implementation:
[list]
[*]A new cosave is written to a sibling temporary file.
[*]The previous cosave is retained while the temporary file is being written.
[*]After the temporary file is complete and closed, Windows replaces the previous cosave with it using write-through replacement.
[*]Cosave loading uses buffered sequential reads of the exact file length.
[/list]

[size=4][b][u]Safety and limitations[/u][/b][/size]
Atomic replacement [b]reduces the risk[/b] that an interrupted save destroys or truncates the previous SKSE cosave. It does not make the .ess and .skse files one indivisible transaction, and it cannot guarantee that every third-party SKSE plugin callback produced logically complete data.

[b]Experimental parallel saving remains disabled by default.[/b] It can run third-party serialization callbacks concurrently, and those callbacks cannot be assumed thread-safe. Leave it disabled unless you understand the risk and have verified your participating plugins.

The plugin does not change the cosave format. No save cleaning or new game is required when installing, updating, or uninstalling it.

[size=4][b][u]Requirements[/u][/b][/size]
[list]
[*][url=https://skse.silverlock.org/]SKSE[/url] or SKSEVR matching your game runtime.
[*]A compatible DLL preloader, such as the preloader supplied with SSE Engine Fixes or DLL Plugin Loader.
[/list]

[size=4][b][u]Installation[/u][/b][/size]
Install with Mod Organizer 2, Vortex, or another FOMOD-capable manager.

[list=1]
[*]Select your Skyrim runtime. The installer should recommend the detected version.
[*]Select [b]Universal (Recommended)[/b] unless you deliberately want the High-End build.
[*]Confirm the installed path is [code]Data\DLLPlugins\Save&LoadAcceleratorForSKSECosaves.dll[/code]
[/list]

[b]Universal x86-64-v2[/b] is the safe default.

[b]High-End x86-64-v3[/b] is intended for AVX2-class CPUs, broadly AMD Zen 2 or newer and Intel Haswell or newer. It may provide little or no measurable improvement depending on the load order and cosave workload. Selecting it on unsupported hardware can cause an illegal-instruction crash.

[size=4][b][u]Compatibility[/u][/b][/size]
The plugin is standalone apart from SKSE and a DLL preloader. It does not require NextGen Disk Cache, and NextGen Disk Cache does not require it. Version 1.2.1 or later of NextGen Disk Cache avoids rewriting save-related file flags and is the intended combination.

Potential conflicts are limited mainly to other DLL plugins that patch the same SKSE save/load routines.

Binaries are supplied for:
[list]
[*]Skyrim AE 1.6.1170, 1.6.1130, 1.6.640, and 1.6.353
[*]Skyrim SE 1.5.97
[*]Skyrim VR 1.4.15
[*]GOG 1.6.1179 and 1.6.659
[/list]
Not every target has been independently runtime-tested by the maintenance fork. Report the exact game version, SKSE version, DLL build, and log when reporting a problem.

[size=4][b][u]Performance[/u][/b][/size]
The original author reported very large reductions in SKSE cosave save and load time on a heavily modded setup. Results vary with cosave size, storage, CPU, configuration, and the behavior of installed SKSE plugins. This mod accelerates only the SKSE sidecar portion of a save, so the total save-screen improvement may be smaller than the cosave-only measurement.

[size=4][b][u]Configuration[/u][/b][/size]
The INI is installed beside the DLL:
[code]Data\DLLPlugins\Save&LoadAcceleratorForSKSECosaves.ini[/code]

The plugin is enabled by default. Timing logs can be configured in the INI. Parallel saving remains off by default and should generally remain off.

[size=4][b][u]Verification[/u][/b][/size]
The release contains:
[list]
[*]the complete public 0BSD source repository on GitHub;
[*]a generated SHA-256 DLL manifest;
[*]an archive SHA-256 sidecar;
[*]a dependency-free Python verifier for manifests, static imports, and PE-section comparisons.
[/list]

The 1.5.1 DLLs can be compared against the corresponding 1.5.0 DLLs. The executable [code].text[/code] section should be identical; differences should be confined to release metadata, the sanitized embedded PDB path, version resources, and the PE checksum.

